```python
class QueueWithStacks:
    def __init__(self):
        self.in_stack = []
        self.out_stack = []

    def enqueue(self, x):
        self.in_stack.append(x)

    def dequeue(self):
        if not self.out_stack:
            while self.in_stack:
                self.out_stack.append(self.in_stack.pop())
        if not self.out_stack:
            raise IndexError("dequeue from empty queue")
        return self.out_stack.pop()
```

Enqueue is always O(1) — a single push onto in-stack, no matter what. Dequeue is O(1) *when the out-stack isn't empty*, and O(n) exactly when it needs a full transfer — but that expensive transfer only happens once per element, ever, which is the seed of the amortized argument in the next slide.

The mirror-image problem: build LIFO access out of FIFO-only containers. One clean approach uses a single queue with rotation, avoiding a second queue entirely.

```python
from collections import deque

class StackWithQueue:
    def __init__(self):
        self.q = deque()

    def push(self, x):
        self.q.append(x)
        for _ in range(len(self.q) - 1):
            self.q.append(self.q.popleft())  # rotate so x is at the front

    def pop(self):
        return self.q.popleft()
```

After every push, the queue is rotated so the newest element sits at the front — exactly where a stack's `pop` needs to look. This makes `push` O(n) and `pop` O(1), the mirror image of the two-stack queue's cost distribution.
